---
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page path="reference/cli">
<section id="commands">
  <h2 id="all"><a href="#all">Every command</a></h2>
  <p>Everything that acts on a universe lives under <code>universe</code>, which <code>u</code> shortens: <code>multiversectl u new 3</code> is <code>multiversectl universe new 3</code>. That is the lifecycle (<code>new</code>, <code>checkout</code>, <code>up</code>, <code>down</code>, <code>rm</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>urls</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>exec</code>) and <code>doctor</code>, which looks after them. What acts on the whole machine rather than on one multiverse lives under <code>system</code>: the shared <code>proxy</code>, <code>prune</code> across every multiverse, the <code>footprint</code> of what this tool put on the disk, and <code>forget</code> and <code>reset</code>. The tree of multiverses and catalogs lives under <code>catalog</code>: an entry is a multiverse or another catalog, and listing, adding, making, removing and updating work the same on both. Starting one is <code>start</code>; there is no separate install. <code>status</code>, <code>server</code>, <code>init</code>, <code>completion</code>, <code>self-update</code> and <code>version</code> stay at the top level. A command's aliases are listed beside it in <code>multiversectl --help</code>.</p>
  <div class="tw entries"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog list [node-path]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Show the catalog as a tree (alias <code>ls</code>; a bare <code>catalog</code> shows its help), or the part under a node. Multiverses already installed are marked, and a layer that cannot be read is listed as unavailable with the reason. <code>--fetch</code> refreshes first, <code>-o json</code> is for scripts, <code>--from</code> shows another catalog. Install what it shows as not installed with <code>multiversectl start &lt;path&gt; --install-only</code>, where the path is the node's path in the tree (a catalog's path installs everything under it), or from a menu with <code>catalog manage</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog list acme/backend</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog add &lt;owner/repo | url | path&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Put a multiverse in the catalog, in the place you pick: a repository with a <code>catalog.yaml</code> is linked as a catalog, one with only a <code>multiverse.yaml</code> as a multiverse; a local checkout is added by its <code>origin</code>; a manifest file goes into a repository (<code>--same-repo</code>, <code>--repo</code>, <code>--new-repo</code>). Where to put it is asked, with the repository each choice writes to; <code>--at</code> names it (a wrong or empty one lists the places), <code>--yes</code> means the root.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog add acme/multiverse</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog rm &lt;path&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Remove an entry from the catalog that holds it. What it points at is untouched, and a multiverse installed from it stays installed.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog rm acme/billing</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe config &lt;universe&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">The manifest a universe uses, checked, and whether it is the one it was started with. Starting a universe records a fingerprint of the manifest (and keeps a copy beside its state), because the file can be edited afterwards and the universe goes on running on what it started with. It lists every problem loading the manifest would meet, and says when the universe was last started, by which build, and whether the manifest has been edited since. <code>--diff</code> shows what changed, <code>--yaml</code> prints the manifest as it is now. The universe's page has the same under Configuration. A universe started before this was kept has no record, and says so.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe config 3 --diff</code></td></tr><tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe hooks &lt;universe&gt;</code>, <code>universe hooks run &lt;universe&gt; &lt;event&gt; [app...]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Lists the commands the manifest ties to a universe's life, and runs an event's now: how a hook that was warned about is run again. A once-only hook runs again and is not marked; a failure is an error.</td></tr><tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe render &lt;universe&gt; [app...]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Renders the files the manifest's <code>render</code> entries describe, as <code>universe up</code> does before it starts an app: from the addresses the universe last started with, writing a file only when its content changed. <code>--print</code> shows the result and writes nothing.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe render 3 api --print</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog manage</code></td><td data-l="What it does">The catalog as a tree on one screen, for a person at a terminal. Move with the arrow keys; <kbd>space</kbd> installs what is not installed or uninstalls what is (on a catalog, everything under it), <kbd>e</kbd> leaves a node out on this machine or brings it back, <kbd>r</kbd> removes the entry from the catalog, <kbd>a</kbd> and <kbd>u</kbd> add an entry or update, <kbd>/</kbd> filters, <kbd>?</kbd> lists every key. Installing, uninstalling and leaving out are staged, shown as you go, and made only when you press <kbd>enter</kbd> and review the list, which shows the command for each (<code>start &lt;path&gt; --install-only</code>, <code>system forget &lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>catalog ignore &lt;path&gt;</code>). Uninstalling only stops tracking it here: its files stay.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog manage</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog ignore &lt;path&gt;...</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Leave a branch of the catalog tree out on this machine. An ignored catalog is not read, so one that is unreachable (behind a VPN, say) costs no wait, and what is under it is left out with it; an ignored multiverse is not offered for installing. A setting of this machine: nothing in any catalog repository changes. The tree marks it, and the web view has an Ignore button on each row.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog ignore acme/vpn-only</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog unignore &lt;path&gt;...</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Bring ignored entries back: they are read and offered again.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog unignore acme/vpn-only</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>system footprint</code></td><td data-l="What it does">List everything multiversectl put on this machine and the command that removes each piece, in a safe order: the web server and proxy that may be running, every universe (with its containers, volumes and worktrees), the clones of your repositories, branches left behind, the configuration and caches, shell completions, and the programs. Anything that would lose work that exists nowhere else, such as uncommitted changes, commits on no remote or a volume's data, is marked. It removes nothing. <code>--script</code> prints only the commands; <code>-o json</code> is for scripts. Run it before <code>system reset</code>, which makes multiverses unknown to it.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl system footprint</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>system forget &lt;multiverse&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Stop tracking a multiverse on this machine; its files stay on disk. Takes the name or the catalog path.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl system forget billing</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>system reset</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Forget this machine's root catalog and every installed multiverse, to start again from another root. Lists them and asks; deletes no file, checkout or universe. Warns about multiverses that still have universes.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl system reset</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog new [dir | name]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Make a multiverse or a catalog and list it; it asks which (<code>--type multiverse|catalog</code>). For a multiverse it guides the setup: choose its repositories (the checkouts beside it, a search through your GitHub or GitLab repositories, or pasted URLs), where its own repository lives (a new private one under any account you can create in, an existing one, or only here), where to list it in your catalog, and whether to start it. Writes <code>multiverse.yaml</code> and an <code>AGENTS.md</code> describing the multiverse and the tool, commits and pushes after you confirm. For a catalog the argument is its name: a file in its parent's repository (<code>--same-repo</code>), an existing repository (<code>--url</code>) or a new private one (<code>--create</code>). Flags answer every question: <code>--name</code>, <code>--repo [name=]url</code> (repeatable; <code>name=</code> sets the name it has in the manifest), <code>--scan</code>, <code>--create owner/name</code> (or a full URL) with <code>--provider github|gitlab</code> and <code>--host</code> for a self-hosted instance, <code>--url</code>, <code>--local</code>, <code>--public</code>, <code>--at</code>, <code>--no-link</code>, <code>--no-install</code>, <code>--yes</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog new --scan --create acme-org/multiverse</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>catalog update [multiverse ...]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Fetch the catalogs, fast-forward each multiverse's manifest from where it was installed, and clone the repositories the new manifest lists (up to four at a time, with a line of progress for each). Refuses a manifest with local changes. It also fast-forwards the shared clones (what secrets are made from), leaving one with changes, on another branch or ahead of origin alone and saying why.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl catalog update</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>schema [field path]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Print what a <code>multiverse.yaml</code> takes, field by field, from the schema this binary was built with — no repository and no network. A path is a field's dotted name, stepping through a list as if it were one item (<code>spec.apps.hosts.port</code>). The field named is spelled out one level down and anything deeper is listed by name under <code>fields</code>, because the whole schema is some 130 KB — a key of its own, since a <code>properties</code> holding names would be an invalid schema that still looked like one. <code>--depth</code> expands more; <code>--depth=-1</code> is the schema itself. <code>--kind catalog</code> is the other file's fields; <code>--json</code> prints the same as JSON.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl schema spec.apps</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe new [number|name]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Create a universe on branches of its own: one worktree per repo, secrets and state. Alias <code>create</code>. In every repository it starts a branch that exists nowhere (in the clone or on origin) from the base; if one exists nothing is created and every clash is listed. With no name the next free number is used, stepping over a number whose branch a removed universe left. <code>--branch</code> (repeatable) names the new branch, a bare name for every repo or <code>repo=name</code>; <code>--base</code> starts it elsewhere, the same way. <code>--note</code> records why it exists.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe new fix/login</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe checkout [branch]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Create a universe on a branch that already exists, to work on or review it. Alias <code>co</code>. It checks the branch out where it exists, named after it (<code>--name</code> for another); a repo without it gets a new branch of that name, or with <code>--only-existing</code> stays on its base, detached. <code>--repo</code> (repeatable) names a different branch for one repo. A branch checked out elsewhere, or that exists nowhere, is refused. With no branch it offers the ones there are. Removing it never deletes the branch.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe checkout fix/login</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe up &lt;universe&gt; [app ...]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Start a universe, all apps or only those named; an app with <code>autostart: false</code> starts only when named. <code>--with</code> adds optional service groups. An app that does not start does not keep the others from it: the rest are started, a summary at the end says which apps are up and which failed and why, and the exit status is non-zero. The hooks of a failed app do not run, those of the apps that started do, and the multiverse's own <code>postUp</code> hooks wait until none failed. <code>--fail-fast</code> stops at the first failure instead. After an app's services start it waits until the ones with a healthcheck are healthy and the hosts that set <code>ready</code> answer, before its <code>postUp</code> hooks: <code>--no-wait</code> skips that, <code>--wait-timeout 10m</code> sets how long (default 5m; running out is a warning), <code>--wait-strict</code> makes running out an error. <code>--rebind-proxy</code> lets the start reconfigure the shared proxy (the interface it is published on, its entrypoints, its TLS) when it differs and universes of other multiverses are running; without it that is refused. All of these are on <code>start</code> too. Alias <code>start</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe up 3 api --with workers</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe down &lt;universe&gt; [app ...]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Stop a universe; keeps data and checkouts. Alias <code>stop</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe down 3</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe note &lt;universe&gt; [text] [--pr url]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Say what a universe is for and which pull or merge request its work is going to. Both are kept with the universe and shown by <code>ls</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>-o json</code> and the web page, so a person or another agent finding it later knows what it is doing. With neither text nor <code>--pr</code> it prints what is there; <code>--clear</code> empties both. <code>universe new --note</code> sets the text when it is made.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe note 3 "fixing the login redirect" --pr https://github.com/acme/api/pull/42</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe notes &lt;universe&gt; [add|set|rm]</code> <span class="muted">(<code>journal</code>)</span></td><td data-l="What it does">Short messages a universe keeps for the user and other agents: a line or two the next reader needs — what is blocked, what is asked of someone, a decision, where things stand — rather than an account of the work, which the commits already are. Reading another universe's notes, or adding one to it, is how agents in different universes reach each other. <code>notes &lt;universe&gt;</code> reads them, <code>add</code> appends one, <code>set</code> replaces what one says and <code>rm</code> takes one out; the id (<code>n1</code>, <code>n2</code>, …) and the time are the tool's to fill in, and <code>--by</code> says who is speaking. Several agents writing at once neither lose nor interleave a note, and a removed note's id is never reused. <code>-</code> as the text reads it from standard input. <code>note</code> is the single line saying what the universe is for. The universe page shows the same notes, and removing the universe removes them.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe notes add 3 --by claude "blocked: needs SMTP creds in .env"</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>help [command] [--tree] [--flat]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Help about one command; with <code>--tree</code>, every command, nested under the one it belongs to, with the aliases each answers to in brackets and a line on what it does. It reads the commands this binary has, so it cannot disagree with it. <code>--flat</code> (which implies <code>--tree</code>) prints one full command per line, to <code>grep</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl help --tree</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>start [name] [source]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">From nothing to something running: installs the source, picks the universe — the one named with <code>-u</code>, the only one there is, or a new one — creates it if it has to, starts it, and prints its addresses. A source is read in this order, and what it was taken for is said: a node in your catalog, a path, a git URL, then <code>owner/repo</code>. A repository may define any number of multiverses: every <code>.yaml</code> or <code>.yml</code> file in it that says <code>kind: Multiverse</code> is one, wherever it is and whatever it is called. <code>start billing owner/repo</code> (or <code>owner/repo:billing</code>) picks one by its <code>metadata.name</code>; a repository with a catalog is read as one, so no root catalog is needed. A repository that defines no multiverse but has a compose file runs as itself, named after the repository (with its owner in front if that name is taken; <code>--as</code> names it). A multiverse already installed is used as it is. With no source it uses the one <code>-m</code> names or the folder you are in, else you pick from your catalog. Where it goes is asked, with <code>~/multiverse/&lt;name&gt;</code> prefilled, unless <code>--root</code>, <code>--base</code> or <code>--yes</code> say. <code>--open</code> opens the first address. Before anything is cloned it checks the machine and says what to run for each problem: docker reachable; compose and buildx present, with their versions; free disk space (refused under 2 GiB); each repository of the multiverse that is not cloned yet is readable (<code>git ls-remote</code>, 15 seconds each, telling no network from no access from an unknown ssh host key); and the secret files the manifest and the compose files (<code>secrets: file:</code>) name are there. A failure stops the start; <code>--skip-preflight</code> starts anyway. The multiverse's own repository is fetched before its list of repositories is known, so it is the one thing checked by the install itself. Several universes and no <code>-u</code> is a question at a terminal and an error in a script. A catalog node installs every multiverse under it: <code>--install-only</code> stops after installing, and <code>--exclude &lt;path&gt;</code> (repeatable) leaves out a multiverse or a whole branch. A catalog repository that does not answer within 20 seconds (<code>MULTIVERSECTL_CATALOG_TIMEOUT</code>) is shown as unavailable, and the rest of the tree stays usable; one fetched before is read from its last copy and marked as not refreshed. Catalogs are read at the same time, and the web view shows each one's state as it answers.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl start acme/multiverse</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe sync [universe]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Bring a universe's code up to date. A universe's checkouts keep the commit they were made from, so a fix that reached <code>main</code> afterwards is not in them. <code>sync</code> fetches and moves each checkout forward when that loses nothing, saying what it did per repository. A checkout with uncommitted changes is never touched; one with commits of its own is left alone unless <code>--merge</code> merges the branch in. <code>--all</code> does every universe. It re-reads the compose labels, so addresses follow; restart the universe to run the new code. <code>universe ls -o wide</code> and <code>universe status</code> say which checkouts are behind.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe sync 1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe rm &lt;universe&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Destroy a universe: worktrees, containers, volumes and state. Uncommitted work in a worktree stops it until <code>--force</code>. Asks first; deletes the branches it started when every commit is elsewhere, and keeps one holding unpushed work; <code>--yes</code> skips. Aliases <code>remove</code>, <code>destroy</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe rm 3 --yes</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe ls</code></td><td data-l="What it does">List universes under a header naming the multiverse, its root, its <code>multiverse.yaml</code>, where it came from and the universes folder. Then a table per universe: a row per address with its app and branch, the forwarded address beside it when there is one, and a row for each repository checked out with no services. <code>-o wide</code> is everything else — the service behind each address, what is on, what is off and why, the host ports it publishes — and an <em>on disk</em> block of worktrees, compose projects and volumes. <code>-o json</code> is a compact one-line document for a script or an agent, <code>-o json,wide</code> the whole detail, <code>-o list</code> one line per universe; they combine with commas. <code>universe ls &lt;universe&gt;</code> shows one. Alias <code>list</code>.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe ls -o wide</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe status</code></td><td data-l="What it does">One line per universe (<code>-o json</code> gives the compact document of <code>ls</code>): whether it is running, partial, stopped or only created, how many of its services are up, when it was last started, its branches and note. <code>ls</code> has the detail per app. A universe is <em>partial</em> when some of the services it expects are not running, and the line under the table says which and why: <em>stopped</em> (exited cleanly), <em>failed</em> (exited with an error, is restarting or dead; with the runtime's own words) or <em>not started</em>. Only what should be up is expected: an app on demand while none of it runs, an app whose repository has no compose file (<em>no runtime</em>), and a service that is optional, deselected or behind a profile are not counted, and the line names the apps it left out. <code>-o json</code> carries the same as <code>why</code> and <code>skipped</code>; <code>ls -o wide</code> prints the line too.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe status</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe urls &lt;universe&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Print a universe's URLs, one per line.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe urls 3</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>server start|stop|restart|status|open|pair</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Run the web view in the background, end it, restart it on the installed build (same options, same link), say where it is, or <code>open</code> it in the browser (starting it first if needed). The view is the separate <code>multiversectl-server</code> program, found beside this one or on the <code>PATH</code>; it is live and can start, stop, create and remove universes, and opens from a link carrying a token that <code>start</code> and <code>status</code> print. <code>start</code> takes <code>--addr</code> (port <code>0</code> picks a free one, and a port another process holds is replaced by the next free one), <code>--open</code>, <code>--read-only</code> and <code>--expose</code> (listen on every interface, for another machine on the network). <code>pair</code> gives that machine a six-digit code to open the page with instead of the link: one machine, once, five minutes.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl server start --open</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>status</code></td><td data-l="What it does">How this machine stands, and what needs attention first: the root catalog (and whether it is marked and readable), each installed multiverse with its drift and how many universes it has and how many run, the shared proxy and the web server, anything <code>doctor</code> warns about, and universes running without the proxy. Offline and quick; <code>--fetch</code> compares with the remotes and looks for a newer release; <code>-o json</code> is for scripts. <code>universe status</code> has the detail of each universe.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl status --fetch</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>system prune</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Remove universes across every installed multiverse (<code>-m</code> narrows it to one), the way <code>universe rm</code> does. It lists what it will do and keeps, saying why, those with containers running (<code>--all</code> includes them) and those with uncommitted work in a worktree (<code>--force</code> discards it). It asks first; <code>--yes</code> skips the question and <code>--dry-run</code> shows the list and stops. The shared clones, the proxy and the installed multiverses are left alone, and a branch a universe started is deleted only when every commit on it is elsewhere.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl system prune --all --yes</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe doctor [universe]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Check the host, the multiverse and optionally a universe; exits 1 on any failure. It warns about a shared clone that is behind its remote and says what to run, and checks each address the way its host speaks (<code>probe</code>). <code>--preflight</code> asks what a first start needs from this machine instead, with no universe and nothing cloned: the same docker, compose, buildx, disk, repository and secret checks <code>start</code> makes, for the installed multiverse when there is one.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe doctor 3</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe exec &lt;universe&gt; &lt;app&gt; -- &lt;cmd&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Run a command in a running container of a universe. <code>--service</code> picks one when the app has several. Streams straight to your terminal.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe exec 3 api --service db -- psql -U postgres</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe run &lt;universe&gt; &lt;app&gt; -- &lt;cmd&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Run a command in a fresh one-off container instead, for a task that should not need the service up — a migration, a shell on a stopped app.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl universe run 3 api -- python manage.py migrate</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>universe env &lt;universe&gt;</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Print a universe's environment as <code>export</code> lines for your shell.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>eval "$(multiversectl universe env 3)"</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>system proxy up|down|status</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Start, stop, or inspect the shared proxy and its network. Universes keep their data when it stops.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl system proxy status</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>init [owner/repo | url | path]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Set up this machine's root catalog. It says what the machine already has, offers to keep it, and checks it is a <code>Catalog</code> and tagged with the topic <code>multiversectl-catalog</code>. On a machine with nothing it searches your GitHub and GitLab repositories for a tagged root, or takes an existing repository or path, a new <strong>private</strong> repository (<code>--create</code>, under any account you choose) or a local git repository (<code>--local</code>). It refuses to switch roots while multiverses installed from the current one exist; <code>system reset</code> first.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl init --create multiverse-index</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>self-update</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Replace this binary, and the web view beside it, with the latest release, and restart the web view if it is running. A release is published only once its binaries are attached, but a tag can still be ahead of them for a moment: when the newest version has none to download yet, it says <em>version X has no binaries attached yet</em> instead of reporting that it is up to date, and a release that has no binary for this platform says so. Commands run in a terminal end with a line on stderr when a newer release is out, with how many fixes it brings; the look is made in the background at most every 30 minutes, so it shows from the second command on. <code>MULTIVERSECTL_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> turns it off. The web view shows the same as a banner.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl self-update</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>version</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Print version, commit, build date and Go version.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl version</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>agents skill install|uninstall|status|show</code></td><td data-l="What it does"><code>install</code> writes a short skill for Claude Code to <code>~/.claude/skills/multiversectl/SKILL.md</code> (<code>--here</code> for this folder's <code>.claude/skills</code>, <code>--dir</code> for another). It tells an agent how to discover the tool (<code>--json</code>), to work in a universe of its own and leave a note and PR on it, and what to ask about first: other universes, <code>prune</code>, <code>system reset</code>, secrets, the shared proxy. The file records the version that wrote it and any newer multiversectl rewrites its own copies, so an upgrade never leaves an agent on old advice; a file it did not write is never touched. <code>status</code> says where it is and whether it is current, <code>show</code> prints it.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl agents skill install</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Command"><code>completion [shell]</code></td><td data-l="What it does">Install shell completions for bash, zsh or fish under your home directory, after asking (<code>--yes</code> skips; <code>--shell</code> overrides <code>$SHELL</code>; <code>--uninstall</code> removes them). With a shell's name, including <code>powershell</code>, prints the script instead. An installed script records the version that wrote it, and any later run of multiversectl rewrites it when the version has changed. Multiverse names and catalog paths complete from what is already on this machine, with no network.</td><td data-l="Example"><code>multiversectl completion --yes</code></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>In <code>--help</code> the commands are grouped: universe management, tool initialization, and catalog management. Run with no command, <code>multiversectl</code> shows the help and, on a machine with nothing installed, the two ways to begin.</p>
  <p>Global flags: <code>-f, --manifest</code> path to the manifest; <code>-m, --multiverse</code> which installed multiverse to act on (default: the one whose folder you are in, else the only one installed); <code>--root</code> multiverse root (default <code>$MULTIVERSECTL_ROOT</code>, the manifest's <code>spec.root</code>, the saved config, or the manifest's directory); <code>--no-color</code>; <code>-v, --verbose</code> says what is being done (<code>-v</code> the steps and what the commands print, <code>-vv</code> the commands run, <code>-vvv</code> how each ended); <code>--json</code> machine-readable output on any command (<code>--less</code> for the least, <code>--full</code> with <code>--help</code> for every command) (see <a href="/docs/tasks/server/#hw-agents">agents</a>). <code>&lt;universe&gt;</code> is a positive integer. <code>--state-dir &lt;dir&gt;</code> (or <code>$MULTIVERSECTL_STATE_DIR</code>) keeps every per-user file under that directory instead of your home: the configuration, the catalog and update caches, the web view's token and state, the shared proxy's compose file (under a project name of its own, so a rehearsal cannot recreate the proxy your universes use), the shell completion and agent skill files, and the default place multiverses are installed (<code>&lt;dir&gt;/home/multiverse</code>). It is how a first run is rehearsed on a machine that has already been used. What it starts lives outside the directory, in Docker: undo it with the same flag, <code>multiversectl --state-dir &lt;dir&gt; universe rm &lt;universe&gt;</code> for each universe and <code>multiversectl --state-dir &lt;dir&gt; system proxy down</code>, then delete the directory. Its proxy needs the same ports as your real one, so only one of the two runs at a time: stop the real one first (<code>multiversectl system proxy down</code>), and start it again after.</p>
